Kernelized Activation Steering Report GitHub Issue × Title: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below: Description: arXiv is now an independent nonprofit! Learn more × Back to arXiv License: CC BY 4.0 arXiv:2610.01062v1 [cs.LG] 01 Oct 2026 Kernelized Activation Steering Laziz U. Abdullaev † † thanks: Equal contribution. Correspondence to laziz.abdullaev@u.nus.edu Affiliation: National University of Singapore Email: laziz.abdullaev@u.nus.edu Minh-Hieu Pham 1 1 footnotemark: 1 Affiliation: Hanoi University of Science and Technology Email: hieu.pm220062@sis.hust.edu.vn Bach Do Affiliation: Hanoi University of Science and Technology Email: bach.dtg225472@sis.hust.edu.vn Khoat Than Affiliation: Hanoi University of Science and Technology Email: khoattq@soict.hust.edu.vn Tan M. Nguyen Affiliation: National University of Singapore Email: tanmn@nus.edu.sg Abstract Activation steering provides a simple, training-free mechanism for controlling attributes of generative models such as sentiment, style, and helpfulness. However, standard approaches such as Difference-in-Means apply a single input-independent steering vector across all activations, limiting expressivity and ignoring the local geometry of the activation space. We propose Kernelized Activation Steering (KAS), a unifying framework that lifts activation steering into a reproducing kernel Hilbert space. KAS formulates steering as an optimization problem expressed purely via kernel evaluations, yielding an implicit, activation-dependent steering score without constructing explicit feature maps. Unlike DiM, KAS induces locally adaptive steering: each activation is modified according to its relative position with respect to source and target reference sets, producing a nonlinear steering field over the representation space. Importantly, DiM is recovered as a special case under a linear kernel, while richer kernels enable geometry-aware interventions. Across standard activation steering tasks, including jailbreaking LLMs and image style control, KAS outperforms or is on par with the existing methods. 1 Introduction Large language models (LLMs) are now known to encode rich semantic concepts such as sentiment, refusal, truthfulness, persona as identifiable structures in their internal representations ( Tigges et al., 2024 ; Marks and Tegmark, 2024 ; Arditi et al., 2024 ) . A central problem in alignment and controllability is to manipulate these structures at inference time , in order to alter behaviour without the cost or fragility of full fine-tuning. Activation steering ( Turner et al., 2023 ; Rimsky et al., 2024 ; Arditi et al., 2024 ) has emerged as a particularly practical instrument for this purpose: it modifies a single layer’s activations using a precomputed direction and requires no parameter updates. The dominant instantiation of this idea is Difference-in-Means (DiM) ( Rimsky et al., 2024 ; Turner et al., 2023 ; Arditi et al., 2024 ) , which estimates the steering direction as the difference between mean activations on a source dataset (behaviour to suppress) and a target dataset (behaviour to elicit). DiM derives both its appeal and its limitations from the linear representation hypothesis (LRH) ( Park et al., 2024 ; Mikolov et al., 2013 ; Arora et al., 2018 ; Elhage et al., 2022 ) — the assumption that high-level concepts are encoded as linear directions in activation space. While the LRH has proven surprisingly robust for many tasks, mounting evidence highlights pervasive polysemanticity and curved structure in transformer representations ( Engels et al., 2025 ; Elhage et al., 2022 ) , where features cluster along nonlinear manifolds that no single global translation can faithfully capture. As a consequence, DiM-style interventions can be either ineffective on out-of-distribution activations or destructive of unrelated capabilities once the steering coefficient is increased to compensate. We propose Kernelized Activation Steering (KAS) , a framework that retains the simplicity of DiM while relaxing its linear constraint via the kernel trick. We lift activations into a reproducing kernel Hilbert space (RKHS) ℋ \mathcal{H} , define the feature-space steering direction as the lifted DiM vector, and derive an implicit steering score that depends on the input only through kernel evaluations against the reference sets. Steering then proceeds by gradient ascent on this score with a proximal regulariser that bounds deviation from the original activation. Because kernel gradients themselves depend on the current activation, the resulting update is locally adaptive: KAS attracts activations toward target reference points and repels them from source reference points, producing a nonlinear steering field (see Figure 1 ). Crucially, KAS recovers DiM exactly under a linear kernel, situating it as a strict generalization rather than an alternative. Overall, our contributions are threefold: 1. A nonlinear generalization of activation steering. We formulate activation steering as optimization in an RKHS and derive an implicit steering score that requires only kernel evaluations between the input and the source/target reference sets (§ 4 ). Furthermore, DiM is recovered exactly under the linear kernel (Remark 4.1 ), unifying classical kernel methods with modern activation-level interventions. 2. A theoretical bridge between activation steering and kernel two-sample test. We show that the KAS score is the empirical witness function associated with maximum mean discrepancy, connecting activation steering to kernel two-sample testing and explaining how characteristic kernels can detect distributional differences that linear mean-based steering misses. 3. A locally adaptive RBF instantiation. We instantiate the framework with the Gaussian RBF kernel, yielding a closed-form, activation-dependent gradient update (§ 4.3 ). We evaluate KAS on jailbreak steering across three instruction-tuned LLMs, CAA-style behavioral steering, and text-to-image style control. KAS improves jailbreak attack success over ActAdd on Gemma and Qwen while matching it on Llama, achieves the strongest effects on four CAA behaviors with non-null steering responses, and improves style induction over Mean-AcT across most tested intervention strengths while preserving prompt alignment. Additional ablations examine kernel approximation and steering from a single contrastive pair (§ 5 ). Figure 1: An illustrative comparison between standard Difference-in-Means (DiM) and Kernelized Activation Steering (KAS). Source activations (orange) lie between two target modes (blue). (a) DiM applies a single global translation 𝒗 = μ B − μ A  m{v}=\mu_{B}-\mu_{A} , sending every activation into the saddle between the modes, a region with no semantic content under either target behaviour. No choice of steering strength α \alpha recovers the modes. (b) Kernelized activation steering (KAS) produces a data-adaptive vector field that routes each activation to its nearest mode. 2 Preliminaries Notation. We write [ n ] := { 1 , … , n } [n]:=\{1,\ldots,n\} for any positive integer n n . For a decoder-only transformer ( Vaswani et al., 2017 ) , we denote by 𝒙 ( ℓ ) ∈ ℝ d  m{x}^{(\ell)}\in\mathbb{R}^{d} the residual-stream activation at layer ℓ ∈ [ L ] \ell\in[L] for a given input prompt. Activation Steering via Difference-in-Means. Activation steering modifies a model’s behaviour at inference time by directly intervening on internal activations, without updating parameters. Given a contrastive pair of prompt sets — a source set A A exhibiting behaviour to suppress and a target set B B exhibiting behaviour to elicit — the difference-in-means (DiM) method ( Rimsky et al., 2024 ; Turner et al., 2023 ; Arditi et al., 2024 ; Venhoff et al., 2025 ) extracts a steering vector by contrasting their mean activations: 𝒗 = 1 n B ​ ∑ j ∈ [ n B ] 𝒃 j − 1 n A ​ ∑ i ∈ [ n A ] 𝒂 i ,  m{v}= rac{1}{n_{B}}\sum_{j\in[n_{B}]} m{b}_{j}- rac{1}{n_{A}}\sum_{i\in[n_{A}]} m{a}_{i}, (1) where { 𝒂 i } i ∈ [ n A ] \{ m{a}_{i}\}_{i\in[n_{A}]} and { 𝒃 j } j ∈ [ n B ] \{ m{b}_{j}\}_{j\in[n_{B}]} are the layer- ℓ ∗ \ell^{*} activations of prompts in A A and B B . At inference, an incoming activation 𝒙  m{x} is steered by the additive update 𝒙 ← 𝒙 + α ​ 𝒗 ,  m{x}\;\leftarrow\; m{x}+\alpha\, m{v}, (2) where α ∈ ℝ \alpha\in\mathbb{R} controls the intervention strength. The intervention layer ℓ ∗ \ell^{*} is typically chosen on a held-out validation set ( Arditi et al., 2024 ) . A defining property of DiM is that the steering direction 𝒗  m{v} is global and input-independent : every activation receives the same translation, regardless of where it sits in representation space. Our work generalises this construction to allow activation-dependent steering through a kernelised formulation. Kernel Methods and Reproducing Kernel Hilbert Spaces. A function k : 𝒳 × 𝒳 → ℝ k:\mathcal{X}	imes\mathcal{X}	o\mathbb{R} is a positive semi-definite (PSD) kernel if, for any finite set { 𝒙 i } i = 1 n ⊂ 𝒳 \{ m{x}_{i}\}_{i=1}^{n}\subset\mathcal{X} , the Gram matrix K ∈ ℝ n × n K\in\mathbb{R}^{n	imes n} with entries K i ​ j = k ⁡ ( 𝒙 i , 𝒙 j ) K_{ij}=k( m{x}_{i}, m{x}_{j}) is positive semi-definite. By the Moore–Aronszajn theorem ( Aronszajn, 1950 ; Berlinet and Thomas-Agnan, 2004 ) , every PSD kernel uniquely defines a reproducing kernel Hilbert space (RKHS) ℋ \mathcal{H} together with a feature map φ : 𝒳 → ℋ \varphi:\mathcal{X}	o\mathcal{H} satisfying k ⁡ ( 𝒙 , 𝒚 ) = ⟨ φ ⁡ ( 𝒙 ) , φ ⁡ ( 𝒚 ) ⟩ ℋ k( m{x}, m{y})\;=\;\langle\varphi( m{x}),\,\varphi( m{y})angle_{\mathcal{H}} for all 𝒙 , 𝒚 ∈ 𝒳  m{x}, m{y}\in\mathcal{X} . The kernel trick states that any algorithm expressible solely through inner products in ℋ \mathcal{H} can be implemented using only kernel evaluations on 𝒳 \mathcal{X} , without materializing φ \varphi . This is what enables us to operate in a high- or infinite-dimensional feature space at the cost of 𝒪 ⁡ ( n ) \mathcal{O}(n) kernel evaluations per query ( Schölkopf and Smola, 2002 ; Hofmann et al., 2008 ) . Kernel methods are usually favorable as it is believed that most nonlinear systems behave approximately linearly in higher, possibly infinite-dimensional feature spaces ( Cover, 1965 ; Bouvrie and Hamzi, 2017 ) . 3 Related Work Activation-level interventions on language models have been studied under several names, including activation addition ( Turner et al., 2023 ) , representation engineering ( Zou et al., 2023a ) , contrastive activation addition ( Rimsky et al., 2024 ) , and inference-time intervention ( Li et al., 2023 ) . Most of these methods extract a single steering direction per concept via difference-in-means, linear probing, or PCA on contrastive activations ( Alain and Bengio, 2016 ; Li et al., 2023 ; Zou et al., 2023a ; Teo et al., 2025 ) . A separate line of work uses sparse autoencoders to identify steerable features ( Templeton et al., 2024 ; O’Brien et al., 2024 ; Bayat et al., 2025 ; Kantamneni et al., 2025 ; Xie, 2025 ) . However, most of these approaches apply a fixed translation in activation space and thus inherit the limitations of input-independent steering. To address this issue, there is a growing body of work dedicated to designing input-dependent steering mechanisms. K-steer ( Oozeer et al., 2025 ) trains a nonlinear attribute classifier on hidden activations and computes steering directions via gradients of that classifier while SAS ( Bayat et al., 2025 ) relies on pretrained SAEs as nonlinear feature extractors. Another line of research models activation steering via Optimal Transport ( Rodriguez et al., 2024 ) through which a numerous input-dependent affine ( Singh et al., 2024 ) and nonlinear ( Abdullaev et al., 2026 ) steering methods have been proposed. Perhaps the closest to our method is Curveball steering ( Raval et al., 2026 ) , which uses kernel PCA for nonlinear dimensionality reduction and perform steering in the reduced space while ( Saillenfest and Lemberger, 2025 ) employs a similar technique for concept erasure problem ( Ravfogel et al., 2020 ; Belrose et al., 2023 ; Chowdhury et al., 2025 ) . More broadly, kernel methods have been used for distribution matching in generative modeling ( Li et al., 2015 ) , particle-based variational inference ( Liu and Wang, 2016 ) , and mode seeking in diffusion-based image restoration ( Hu et al., 2025 ) , providing related distributional perspectives. Our training-free framework, Kernelized Activation Steering (KAS), instead derives an activation-dependent intervention from an interpretable, kernel-based optimization objective and recovers DiM exactly as a special case under a linear kernel, along with a relation to hypothesis testing techniques ( Gretton et al., 2012 ; Muandet et al., 2017 ) providing a complementary theoretical lens for activation steering design. 4 Kernelized Activation Steering Let { 𝒂 i } i ∈ [ n A ] \{ m{a}_{i}\}_{i\in[n_{A}]} and { 𝒃 j } j ∈ [ n B ] \{ m{b}_{j}\}_{j\in[n_{B}]} denote the layer- ℓ ∗ \ell^{*} activations of prompts drawn from a source set A A and target set B B , respectively. The DiM steering vector ( 1 ) is effective when A A and B B are well separated by a linear hyperplane in activation space, but totally disregards local structures of the activation distributions and possible nonlinearities in decision boundary. To approach these issues properly, we shall derive DiM activation steering as a special case of a more general, principled operation in the following section. 4.1 Lifting Difference-in-Means into an RKHS Let φ : ℝ d → ℋ \varphi:\mathbb{R}^{d}	o\mathcal{H} be the feature map associated with a PSD kernel k k , where ℋ \mathcal{H} may be infinite-dimensional. The natural lift of ( 1 ) into ℋ \mathcal{H} is 𝒗 φ = 1 n B ​ ∑ j ∈ [ n B ] φ ⁡ ( 𝒃 j ) − 1 n A ​ ∑ i ∈ [ n A ] φ ⁡ ( 𝒂 i ) .  m{v}_{\varphi}\;=\; rac{1}{n_{B}}\sum_{j\in[n_{B}]}\varphi( m{b}_{j})\;-\; rac{1}{n_{A}}\sum_{i\in[n_{A}]}\varphi( m{a}_{i}). (3) For sufficiently expressive kernels, for example, universal kernels such as the Gaussian RBF ( Steinwart and Christmann, 2008 ) , the contrastive features of the lifted samples { φ ⁡ ( 𝒂 i ) } \{\varphi( m{a}_{i})\} and { φ ⁡ ( 𝒃 j ) } \{\varphi( m{b}_{j})\} are expected to be captured well in ℋ \mathcal{H} , motivating 𝒗 φ  m{v}_{\varphi} as a steering direction in the feature space. Materialising 𝒗 φ  m{v}_{\varphi} explicitly is, however, infeasible when ℋ \mathcal{H} is very high or infinite-dimensional. We circumvent this by expressing the entire steering objective in terms of kernel evaluations as we discuss next. Ideally, for an activation 𝒙 0 ∈ ℝ d  m{x}_{0}\in\mathbb{R}^{d} , we would like the steered activation 𝒙  m{x} to satisfy the equation φ ⁡ ( 𝒙 ) − φ ⁡ ( 𝒙 0 ) = η ​ 𝒗 φ \varphi( m{x})-\varphi( m{x}_{0})=\eta m{v}_{\varphi} for some η > 0 \eta>0 , which represents that the feature-space steering direction is the same as 𝒗 φ  m{v}_{\varphi} . Motivated by this, we design the feature-space steering mechanism to increase the dot product similarity ⟨ φ ⁡ ( 𝒙 ) − φ ⁡ ( 𝒙 0 ) , 𝒗 φ ⟩ \langle\varphi( m{x})-\varphi( m{x}_{0}),\, m{v}_{\varphi}angle with respect to 𝒙  m{x} . As the contribution of φ ⁡ ( 𝒙 0 ) \varphi( m{x}_{0}) to this objective is independent of 𝒙  m{x} , we define the following implicit steering score Π ⁡ ( 𝒙 ) := ⟨ φ ⁡ ( 𝒙 ) , 𝒗 φ ⟩ ℋ . \Pi( m{x})\;:=\;\langle\varphi( m{x}),\, m{v}_{\varphi}angle_{\mathcal{H}}. (4) By bilinearity of the inner product and the kernel-trick identity ⟨ φ ⁡ ( 𝒙 ) , φ ⁡ ( 𝒚 ) ⟩ ℋ = k ⁡ ( 𝒙 , 𝒚 ) \langle\varphi( m{x}),\,\varphi( m{y})angle_{\mathcal{H}}=k( m{x}, m{y}) , Π ⁡ ( 𝒙 ) \displaystyle\Pi( m{x}) = ⟨ φ ⁡ ( 𝒙 ) , 1 n B ​ ∑ j ∈ [ n B ] φ ⁡ ( 𝒃 j ) − 1 n A ​ ∑ i ∈ [ n A ] φ ⁡ ( 𝒂 i ) ⟩ \displaystyle=\langle\varphi( m{x}),\, rac{1}{n_{B}}\sum_{j\in[n_{B}]}\varphi( m{b}_{j})\;-\; rac{1}{n_{A}}\sum_{i\in[n_{A}]}\varphi( m{a}_{i})angle = 1 n B ​ ∑ j ∈ [ n B ] k ⁡ ( 𝒙 , 𝒃 j ) − 1 n A ​ ∑ i ∈ [ n A ] k ⁡ ( 𝒙 , 𝒂 i ) , \displaystyle= rac{1}{n_{B}}\sum_{j\in[n_{B}]}k( m{x}, m{b}_{j})\;-\; rac{1}{n_{A}}\sum_{i\in[n_{A}]}k( m{x}, m{a}_{i}), (5) which depends only on kernel evaluations between 𝒙  m{x} and the reference sets. Intuitively, Π ⁡ ( 𝒙 ) \Pi( m{x}) measures the alignment of φ ⁡ ( 𝒙 ) \varphi( m{x}) with the lifted DiM direction 𝒗 φ  m{v}_{\varphi} . Therefore, increasing Π \Pi pushes the feature representation of 𝒙  m{x} toward the the target set and away from that of the source set. 4.2 Steering as an Optimization Problem Given an initial activation 𝒙 0 ∈ ℝ d  m{x}_{0}\in\mathbb{R}^{d} , we cast kernelized steering as the regularized maximization problem given by 𝒙 ∗ = arg ​ max 𝐱 ∈ ℝ d ⁡ F ​ ( 𝐱 ) , F ⁡ ( 𝐱 ) := Π ⁡ ( 𝐱 ) − λ 2 ​ ∥ 𝐱 − 𝐱 0 ∥ 2 ,  m{x}^{*}\;=\;\argmax_{ m{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}}\;F( m{x})\,,\qquad F( m{x})\;:=\;\Pi( m{x})\;-\; rac{\lambda}{2}\,\lVert m{x}- m{x}_{0}Vert^{2}, (6) where λ ≥ 0 \lambda\geq 0 is a proximal coefficient that optionally prevents the steered activation from drifting too far from 𝒙 0  m{x}_{0} and thereby disrupting unrelated capabilities of the model. When k k is differentiable, we approximate 𝒙 ∗  m{x}^{*} by gradient ascent on F F : 𝒙 ← 𝒙 + η ⁡ [ 1 n B ​ ∑ j ∈ [ n B ] ∇ 𝒙 k ​ ( 𝒙 , 𝒃 j ) − 1 n A ​ ∑ i ∈ [ n A ] ∇ 𝒙 k ​ ( 𝒙 , 𝒂 i ) − λ ⁡ ( 𝒙 − 𝒙 0 ) ] , \displaystyle m{x}\;\leftarrow\; m{x}+\eta\!\left[ rac{1}{n_{B}}\sum_{j\in[n_{B}]}
