oposition formalizes this equivalence. Proposition 4.3 (KAS as MMD witness ascent) . Let q A q_{A} and q B q_{B} denote the source and target activation distributions, with mean embeddings μ A , μ B ∈ ℋ \mu_{A},\mu_{B}\in\mathcal{H} . The population steering score Π ∞ ​ ( 𝐱 ) := 𝔼 𝐛 ∼ q B ​ [ k ⁡ ( 𝐱 , 𝐛 ) ] − 𝔼 𝐚 ∼ q A ​ [ k ⁡ ( 𝐱 , 𝐚 ) ] \Pi^{\infty}( m{x})\;:=\;\mathbb{E}_{ m{b}\sim q_{B}}[k( m{x}, m{b})]-\mathbb{E}_{ m{a}\sim q_{A}}[k( m{x}, m{a})] is the unnormalized maximum mean discrepancy (MMD) witness function for the two-sample problem q B q_{B} vs. q A q_{A} ( Gretton et al., 2012 ) . Concretely, Π ∞ ​ ( 𝒙 ) = MMD ⁡ [ ℋ , q B , q A ] ⋅ f ∗ ​ ( 𝒙 ) , \Pi^{\infty}( m{x})\;=\;\mathrm{MMD}[\mathcal{H},q_{B},q_{A}]\cdot f^{*}( m{x}), where f ∗ ∈ ℋ f^{*}\in\mathcal{H} , ‖ f ∗ ‖ ℋ = 1 \|f^{*}\|_{\mathcal{H}}=1 is a witness function that attains sup ‖ f ‖ ℋ ≤ 1 [ 𝔼 B ​ f − 𝔼 A ​ f ] \sup_{\|f\|_{\mathcal{H}}\leq 1}[\mathbb{E}_{B}f-\mathbb{E}_{A}f] . The empirical KAS score Π \Pi in ( 5 ) is the standard plug-in estimator of Π ∞ \Pi^{\infty} , and Algorithm 2 performs gradient ascent on this empirical witness. Identifying Π \Pi with the MMD witness imports a couple of useful properties from kernel two-sample testing. (i) Characteristic kernels and signal existence. When k k is characteristic on ℝ d \mathbb{R}^{d} (true for the Gaussian RBF and Laplace kernels ( Gretton et al., 2012 ; Sriperumbudur et al., 2010 ) ), Π ∞ ≡ 0 \Pi^{\infty}\equiv 0 if and only if q A = q B q_{A}=q_{B} . In contrast, a linear kernel yields Π ∞ ≡ 0 \Pi^{\infty}\equiv 0 iff the means of the two distributions agree, so DiM has no population signal whenever source and target activations share a centroid but differ in higher moments (e.g. multimodal targets). This formalizes DiM’s principal failure mode and motivates the use of nonlinear kernels in KAS. (ii) Bandwidth selection. The MMD median heuristic, setting σ \sigma to the median pairwise Euclidean distance in A ∪ B A\cup B , gives a pa