lexity cap. These comparisons are restricted to the evaluated configurations. 5.2 CAA-style Contrastive Steering on Multiple-choice Question Answering Setup. We adopt the contrastive multiple-choice setup of CAA ( Rimsky et al., 2024 ) . Each pair shares a question and differs only in the appended answer: the positive example selects the behavior-consistent option and the negative example selects the opposite option. Activations are collected at the answer-token position. For each behavior, we retain 50 held-out test questions, remove duplicates and test-set overlaps from the generation pool, sample 50 validation questions, and use the remaining examples as references. Layer and strength are selected by maximizing behavior-consistent answer probability on the validation set; the selected configuration is then evaluated on the test set. Experiments use Gemma-2-9B-IT at layer 22 or 23. We consider no system prompt, a positive system prompt encouraging the behavior, and a negative system prompt discouraging it. Table 2 reports the negative-prompt condition, comparing the unsteered model, linear contrastive addition (denoted ActAdd), and the methods in Section 5.1 . The reported standard deviations follow the same three-seed convention. Results. KAS exceeds linear contrastive addition by 9.63, 11.04, and 8.92 percentage points on Corrigibility, Hallucination, and Myopic Reward, respectively. KAS attains the highest probability among the compared methods on AI Coordination, Corrigibility, Hallucination, and Myopic Reward. Survival Instinct and Sycophancy remain close to the unsteered baseline and are treated as near-null effects. Table 2: CAA-style steering under a negative system prompt. Entries report the mean probability (%) of the behavior-consistent answer at validation-selected settings; ± \pm denotes standard deviation over three seeds. The final two behaviors exhibit near-null effects. Behavior Unsteered ActAdd Curveball CHaRS KAS AI Coordination 8.69 39.51 27.48