e induction and content preservation for the cyberpunk style. KAS achieves higher 0-shot style-classification scores than Mean-AcT across most intervention strengths. Notably, near λ = 1 \lambda=1 , KAS reaches a high zero-shot style score while maintaining a high CLIPScore , indicating effective style induction without reducing prompt alignment. The qualitative results in Figure 4 show the same trend: stronger interventions introduce more cyberpunk visual attributes. Table 3: Kernel-approximation ablation for jailbreak steering. ASR and utility scores are percentages; PPL is Alpaca perplexity. Nyström-KAS uses 64 landmarks, with mean and standard deviation over three sampling seeds. Variant ASR PPL tArc tHella tMMLU tQA tWino Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct KAS 96.00 6.60 62.57 76.97 64.16 65.79 68.30 Nyström-KAS 22.33 ± \pm 0.47 6.54 ± \pm 0.06 64.58 ± \pm 0.00 78.52 ± \pm 0.00 74.36 ± \pm 0.00 52.47 ± \pm 0.15 72.85 ± \pm 0.54 Gemma-2-9B-IT KAS 86.00 10.03 61.42 78.80 72.38 43.96 73.81 Nyström-KAS 54.67 ± \pm 0.94 9.99 ± \pm 0.06 63.15 ± \pm 1.75 78.26 ± \pm 1.15 72.03 ± \pm 0.28 43.88 ± \pm 0.39 73.69 ± \pm 0.20 Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct KAS 94.00 6.81 55.88 70.47 65.40 53.54 72.85 Nyström-KAS 3.00 ± \pm 0.82 6.46 ± \pm 0.01 62.74 ± \pm 0.00 82.17 ± \pm 0.37 62.98 ± \pm 0.19 52.84 ± \pm 0.09 72.29 ± \pm 0.75 Table 4: Kernel-approximation ablation for CAA-style steering under a negative system prompt. Nyström-KAS uses 64 landmarks and three sampling seeds. Behavior KAS Nyström-KAS AI Coordination 40.19 42.18 ± \pm 1.39 Corrigibility 43.45 51.80 ± \pm 1.86 Hallucination 55.01 54.13 ± \pm 0.09 Myopic Reward 58.48 55.26 ± \pm 0.86 Survival Instinct 43.83 43.76 ± \pm 0.03 Sycophancy 53.69 53.23 ± \pm 0.06 5.4 Ablation: Kernel Approximation Setup. We examine the effect of approximating the KAS kernel using the Nyström method ( Williams and Seeger, 2000 ) with 64 landmarks sampled without replacement from the reference sets. Appendix A.4 derives the approximate score and steering update. We compare this variant, denoted Nyström-KAS, with exact KAS under the reference, validation, and test protocols of Sections 5.1 and 5.2 . Each variant uses its own validation-selected layer and strength; the comparison therefore measures performance after selection rather than approximation error at a fixed intervention. Nyström results report mean and standard deviation over three landmark-sampling seeds. Results. Table 3 shows that the approximation reduces jailbreak ASR on all three models, with slightly lower Alpaca perplexity. In contrast, Table 4 shows that the approximation improves AI Coordination and Corrigibility, while exact KAS achieves higher probabilities on Hallucination and Myopic Reward. Both variants exhibit near-null effects on Survival Instinct and Sycophancy. Thus, the effect of kernel approximation depends on the task; these results do not establish a uniform substitute for exact kernel evaluation or quantify computational savings. 5.5 Ablation: Jailbreaking from a Single Contrastive Pair Figure 5: Single-pair jailbreak steering across three instruction-tuned models. Box plots report attack success rate (ASR), Pile perplexity, and Alpaca perplexity over ten randomly sampled harmful–harmless contrastive prompt pairs. Steering is applied at layer 22 for Gemma-2-9B-IT , layer 12 for Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct , and layer 18 for Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct . KAS is evaluated with one gradient step and ten gradient steps using the step sizes shown on the x-axis, and is compared against ActAdd. We further evaluate whether jailbreak steering can be induced from an extremely limited contrastive set. In this ablation, each steering direction is extracted from only one harmful–harmless prompt pair. We repeat this procedure over ten randomly sampled contrastive pairs and evaluate using the same protocol as in Section 5.1 . To quantify the extent each steering method maintains general capabilities of the model, we also measure perplexity on the Pile ( Gao et al., 2020 ) and Alpaca ( Taori et al., 2023 ) datasets. This setting provides a stress test for data efficiency: a successful method must be able to recover a useful behavioral direction from a minimal number of contrastive examples while avoiding severe degradation of generation quality. Figure 5 visualizes the distribution of one-shot jailbreak steering performance across ten randomly sampled contrastive prompt pairs. The main trend is that KAS preserves general utility substantially better than ActAdd while remaining competitive in ASR. On Gemma-2-9B-IT , KAS achieves ASR comparable to ActAdd, with both one-step and ten-step variants attaining similar mean ASR while reducing Pile and Alpaca perplexity. On Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct , KAS remains comparable to ActAdd in ASR, but yields markedly lower perplexity on both utility corpora. On Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct , ActAdd obtains the highest ASR, yet KAS substantially reduces perplexity. Overall, KAS remains effective under this severe data constraint while improving generation stability relative to a global linear intervention. 6 Conclusion We introduced Kernelized Activation Steering (KAS), a unifying framework that recasts activation steering as optimization in a reproducing kernel Hilbert space. By expressing the steering objective entirely through kernel evaluations, KAS produces an implicit, activation-dependent steering field that is locally adaptive to the geometry of the representation manifold, while strictly generalizing the classical Difference-in-Means construction, which is recovered exactly under a linear kernel. We view KAS as a step toward a more principled, geometry-aware approach to inference-time control of generative models, and an opening for further work on kernel design and adaptive layer selection. A limitation of KAS is that the intervention applies a uniform number of gradient ascent steps to every token, which is computationally wasteful for tokens already close to the target manifold and potentially 